    "rationale": "Chose NO_FIX because none of the three strategies can be applied to lines 170-180 alone. The path starts at a cycle_counter flop and ends at a write-pointer flop in wptr_full.v:52; 0.620 ns of the 0.940 ns sits in cells 1-8 (NOR4/NAND4/OAI22/NOR2/AOI211/AND3/AND3/NAND3), the classic shape of a threshold compare on cycle_counter ANDed with the FSM_START/FSM_RECV/FSM_STOP decode that produces next_bit and the FIFO write enable. That cone is not in the scope I was shown. The remaining 0.240 ns (three XOR2 + NOR3) is the binary increment and gray recode inside wptr_full. RESTRUCTURE rejected: the only combinational logic in my range is cycle_counter + 1'b1, which drives the D pin of the startpoint flop and therefore is not on the reported path, so rewriting it changes no stage delay. RETIME rejected: there is no register inside the violating combinational chain - it is a clean flop-to-flop cone spanning two modules - and moving cycle_counter itself would shift the UART bit-sampling instant. PIPELINE rejected: a new stage on cycle_counter or on its compare output would delay next_bit by one cycle, and the reconvergent fsm_state, bit_counter and recv_reg logic that must be rebalanced all lies outside lines 170-180, so the cut would change function, not just timing. A correct fix is latency-neutral: compute the terminal-count flag from (cycle_counter + 1'b1) and register it in this same process, then have next_bit consume that single registered bit instead of the wide compare/decode cone. That needs simultaneous edits to the counter process and the next_bit assignment, the latter being outside the permitted range, so the scope must be widened.",
    "out_of_scope_fix": {
      "src_file": "rtl/vendor/uart/rtl/uart_rx.v",
      "start_line": 170,
      "end_line": 210,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "reason": "The dominant 0.62 ns of the path is the cycle_counter threshold compare ANDed with the FSM state decode that generates next_bit/write-enable, which sits just after the counter process and was not included in the 170-180 window; folding that compare into the registered counter process is latency-neutral but requires editing the next_bit assignment too."
    }
